                    {
                        "form_id": "a5212faa-80cd-420a-93ba-14eee01205ac",
                        "entry_id": "23555cf0-13fe-38a8-97d0-0d22336cfab2",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "gazetteered"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "acc8ed9b-ec8a-320f-988a-78ae02c266e5",
                        "entry_id": "23555cf0-13fe-38a8-97d0-0d22336cfab2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of gazette (“to announce the status of (someone) in an official gazette”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cf59c9fc-f1f1-43ed-8d82-33e81b046dca",
                                "sense_id": "acc8ed9b-ec8a-320f-988a-78ae02c266e5",
                                "text": "[A]s an old friend I've got one favour to beg and to request to be granted. […] Why, when you've been gazetteered as Sir Robert and Lady Smugglefuss, that I shall be the first to be honoured with a visit."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f65438c2-2a60-45ab-a7b0-0d4803a2fc61",
                                "sense_id": "acc8ed9b-ec8a-320f-988a-78ae02c266e5",
                                "text": "But the change was at hand, and two new major-generals and six brigadier-generals were gazetteered to the anxious country."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b8758d20-f973-4007-9dcb-dbf5919576df",
                                "sense_id": "acc8ed9b-ec8a-320f-988a-78ae02c266e5",
                                "text": "Gazetteered as Lieutenant-Commander, he [Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh] was now twenty-nine years old, about the age when he might have expected such promotion. No sign there of favouritism or nepotism, though his unrivalled connections had hardly held him back."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "187aba8a-4e56-3858-b341-bd26522fd04c",
                        "entry_id": "23555cf0-13fe-38a8-97d0-0d22336cfab2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To report about (someone) in a gazette or newspaper."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "22f8b7d4-e9e4-4d9c-8e49-3116a5613c4c",
                                "sense_id": "187aba8a-4e56-3858-b341-bd26522fd04c",
                                "text": "[O]ur modern canibals of the gazetteering tribe, leſs delicate than they, can divert themſelves in cool blood vvith the pangs of their friends, and exert their more licentious brutality amidſt the miſeries of nations in ſtrict alliance vvith their ovvn;—if ſuch inſects can be deem'd of any nation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "103df595-1f23-4461-952e-030d572c61a8",
                                "sense_id": "187aba8a-4e56-3858-b341-bd26522fd04c",
                                "text": "Patience, it will be our turn by and by, we shall have the honour of being Gazetteered in our place, at least I expect a whole paragraph in the 'Evening Post' for my own share."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
