                        "entry_id": "bbbe85c3-a254-34dd-ad75-3160145e72fe",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To describe the geography of (a country or other place) in a gazetteer (etymology 2, noun sense 1)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "63f74517-21bf-4514-b619-e2b4ac136353",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "Such a cosmopolitan collection of post-marks is seldom made. They have a conventional range, from Buckingham Palace and Osborne, through university towns, scholars' libraries, remote parishes in Scotland, the seats of power in British India, to places Down East, and towns at the West not yet gazetteered; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "15f42774-c0b8-4dba-a908-233f9c455419",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "Neither of them could make poetry coalesce with gazetteering or chronicle-making. It was like trying to put a declaration of love into the forms of a declaration in trover."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "04ad9820-390f-49dd-8bc5-5a4a22b3ebba",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "[…] Wressley went back to the Foreign Office and his \"Wajahs,\" a compiling, gazetteering, report-writing hack, who would have been dear at three hundred rupees a month."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "afe93cef-0187-430d-aca6-589d18580f83",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "[…] Peter Underwood […] has carefully and systematically gazetteered ghosts throughout Britain."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6ddbabe1-7828-4c41-89ae-5b9eb64386e1",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "I have a lease, Captain. I paid you a thousand dollars. The river is even gazetteered in my name."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6607cc58-a145-4244-9edf-03720174c519",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "A problem that almost defies solution in such a work is the rendering of place-names on the maps, which in the case of The International Atlas are gazetteered according to the local name and in English, German, Spanish, French, and Portuguese."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ca98dbe9-9800-4b36-8718-c0756ce95dca",
                                "sense_id": "2c459d40-f9ca-3abc-96b8-b6b04f7e54fc",
                                "text": "The success of the gigantic undertaking has been unparalleled: few countries, if any, are more thoroughly gazetteered than India."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
